WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.600
Elena owns a policy on Marcus's life and names their adult

2
00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:05.740
daughter as revocable beneficiary.

3
00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:09.840
Elena controls the policyowner's rights under the contract.

4
00:00:11.220 --> 00:00:15.060
Marcus is the insured, and the daughter is the designated recipient

5
00:00:15.060 --> 00:00:16.700
of a covered death benefit.

6
00:00:17.540 --> 00:00:21.300
The person sending premium payments does not automatically become the owner.

7
00:00:21.980 --> 00:00:26.580
Owner rights can include changing a revocable beneficiary, assigning the

8
00:00:26.580 --> 00:00:31.820
contract, choosing available options and accessing policy values when

9
00:00:31.820 --> 00:00:33.360
the contract provides them.

10
00:00:34.060 --> 00:00:37.720
Each right remains subject to policy terms and other valid interests.

11
00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:42.160
A term policy does not acquire a cash value merely because

12
00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:43.760
its owner asks for a loan.

13
00:00:44.394 --> 00:00:49.194
With a revocable designation, the reserved right to change beneficiaries

14
00:00:49.194 --> 00:00:51.634
ordinarily remains with the owner.

15
00:00:52.514 --> 00:00:57.094
An irrevocable designation creates an interest that can restrict changes

16
00:00:57.094 --> 00:00:59.094
without the required consent.

17
00:00:59.934 --> 00:01:03.734
An existing assignment can also affect what the owner may do.

18
00:01:04.514 --> 00:01:08.874
Maryland permits the policy to require insurer endorsement or acceptance

19
00:01:08.874 --> 00:01:11.914
before a beneficiary change binds the insurer.

20
00:01:11.914 --> 00:01:17.154
A verbal request to a producer is not a reliable substitute

21
00:01:17.154 --> 00:01:19.454
for completing the required process.

22
00:01:20.374 --> 00:01:24.934
Identify the current owner, designation and assignments first,

23
00:01:25.534 --> 00:01:30.254
then obtain the proper documents before describing the change as complete.

24
00:01:31.164 --> 00:01:36.804
Suppose Elena executes a valid absolute assignment to her adult daughter.

25
00:01:37.744 --> 00:01:41.844
An absolute assignment transfers the ownership rights described by that

26
00:01:41.844 --> 00:01:46.124
complete transfer. The daughter becomes the new owner through the required

27
00:01:46.124 --> 00:01:51.524
process. This is different from naming her beneficiary, which only addresses

28
00:01:51.524 --> 00:01:53.604
who receives an applicable benefit.

29
00:01:54.364 --> 00:01:58.004
Changing ownership does not ordinarily change the person whose life is

30
00:01:58.004 --> 00:02:03.024
insured. It also does not erase existing policy limitations, loans

31
00:02:03.024 --> 00:02:07.824
or superior interests. The parties should review the actual assignment and

32
00:02:07.824 --> 00:02:10.124
obtain any required consent or recording.

33
00:02:11.064 --> 00:02:15.384
A transfer can have tax consequences that deserve separate review.

34
00:02:16.278 --> 00:02:21.618
Now assume Elena retains ownership but pledges
specified policy rights to a lender as collateral.

36
00:02:22.438 --> 00:02:26.138
A collateral assignment secures an obligation instead of simply giving the

37
00:02:26.138 --> 00:02:28.518
lender the whole beneficial interest forever.

38
00:02:29.458 --> 00:02:32.738
Read the document: it can grant enforcement rights and limit the

39
00:02:32.738 --> 00:02:34.538
owner's actions while the debt exists.

40
00:02:35.278 --> 00:02:38.313
In a simplified covered death claim, the payable benefit is one

41
00:02:38.313 --> 00:02:41.818
hundred thousand dollars and the secured amount due is twenty thousand

42
00:02:41.818 --> 00:02:44.318
dollars. Assume no other claims or deductions.

43
00:02:45.078 --> 00:02:47.778
The lender receives twenty thousand dollars and the designated beneficiary

44
00:02:47.778 --> 00:02:50.338
receives the remaining eighty thousand dollars.

45
00:02:51.456 --> 00:02:56.036
After the secured obligation is satisfied, obtain the appropriate release of

46
00:02:56.036 --> 00:02:59.916
the collateral assignment and follow the insurer's recording process.

47
00:03:00.536 --> 00:03:04.096
Do not assume the company's records update because the lender cashed

48
00:03:04.096 --> 00:03:08.296
a final payment. An assignment can be subject to an insurer's

49
00:03:08.296 --> 00:03:11.056
prior lien and specific reserved rights.

50
00:03:12.016 --> 00:03:15.696
Its exact wording matters more than a blanket statement that every

51
00:03:15.696 --> 00:03:17.556
assignment transfers every right.

52
00:03:18.256 --> 00:03:22.036
An insurer's acknowledgment generally records the transaction under its

53
00:03:22.036 --> 00:03:25.236
process; it is not a promise that every legal or tax

54
00:03:25.236 --> 00:03:26.896
consequence has been reviewed.

55
00:03:27.656 --> 00:03:31.336
Keep copies and confirm who can exercise each affected right.

56
00:03:31.866 --> 00:03:37.086
Maryland's individual life provision treats the policy, or the policy with

57
00:03:37.086 --> 00:03:42.146
an endorsed or attached application, as the entire contract between the

58
00:03:42.146 --> 00:03:46.306
parties. Where the application is included, its statements are

59
00:03:46.306 --> 00:03:50.166
representations rather than warranties in the absence of fraud.

60
00:03:50.926 --> 00:03:55.286
Required riders and endorsements form part of the actual contract as

61
00:03:55.286 --> 00:03:58.666
incorporated. Sales material is separate from the policy.

62
00:03:58.666 --> 00:04:03.266
A producer cannot amend the coverage through an informal promise.

63
00:04:03.966 --> 00:04:09.646
Check the documents, insured amount, options and accepted changes.

64
00:04:10.366 --> 00:04:14.086
Application copies and corrections also have Maryland rules.

65
00:04:14.846 --> 00:04:18.646
Use the actual contract to resolve rights instead of relying on

66
00:04:18.646 --> 00:04:20.426
a remembered sales conversation.
